<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-sets-cardinality" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>The Number of Elements in a Finite Set</title>

	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use the inclusion-exclusion rule to count the number of elements in the union of two sets.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use a Venn diagram to find the number of elements in each region of a three-set problem.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>
	<introduction>
		<p>
			Counting is a core idea in mathematics, practiced since the dawn of history. Finite sets, with a specific number of distinct elements, are essential to this process. When dealing with two or three sets, we need to track elements that occur in multiple sets. Set operations<mdash/>intersections, unions, and complements<mdash/>are crucial tools for solving these counting problems.
		</p>
	</introduction>
	<subsection>
		<title>Counting Problems with Two Sets</title>
		<p>
			Sometimes we are interested in the number of elements in the union or intersection of sets, but do not know the actual elements of each set. The following examples explore these ideas in problems involving two or three sets.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-sets-cardinality">
			<statement>      
				<p>
					The number of elements in a set is called the <term>cardinality</term><idx>cardinality</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>cardinality</h></idx> of the set. The cardinality of set <m>A</m> is denoted <m>\lvert A \rvert</m> or <m>n(A)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<example>
			<p>
				A survey asks 200 people <q>What beverage do you drink in the morning?</q>, and offers choices:
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							Tea only,
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Coffee only,
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Both coffee and tea,
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Neither.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
				Suppose 20 report tea only, 80 report coffee only, and 40 report both. How many people drink tea in the morning? How many people drink neither tea nor coffee?
			</p>
			<sidebyside widths="50% 45%" margins="2.5% 0%" valign="top">
				<p>
					This question can be most easily answered by creating a Venn diagram.  We see that we can find the people who drink tea by adding those who drink only tea to those who drink both: 60 people.
					We can also see that those who drink neither are those not contained in any of the three other groupings, so we can count those by subtracting from the cardinality of the universal set, 200.  
					<md>
						200 - 20 - 80 - 40 = 60
					</md>
					people who drink neither.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-2venn-coffee-tea" width="100%">
					<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are two overlapping circles inside. One is labeled "Coffee" and contains the number 80. The other is labeled "Tea" and contains the number 20. The intersection of the two circles contains the number 40.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2/7,font=\figfont]
							\draw[semithick] (0,0) rectangle (21,14);
							% Sets: Path is fill, draw is bddry
							\path[fill=white] (7,7) circle (5);
							\path[fill=yellow!50] (14,7) circle (5);
							\draw[semithick] (7,7) circle (5);
							\draw[semithick] (14,7) circle (5);
							% Set names
							\node at (7,1) {Coffee};
							\node at (14,1) {Tea};
							% Numbers in each region
							\node at (6,7) {80};  % Only in X
							\node at (10.5,7) {40};    % Intersection
							\node at (15,7) {20};   % Only in Y
					\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</sidebyside>
		</example>
		<p>
			The next example illustrates again how to deal with overlapping counts and develops an important counting formula.
		</p>
		<example>
			<p>
				Suppose a survey asks participants <q>Which online services have you used in the last month?</q> and offers a choice of
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							X (formerly Twitter),
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Facebook,
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Both X and Facebook,
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Neither.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
				The results show 40% of those surveyed have used X, 70% have used Facebook, and 20% have used both.  What percentage of those surveyed have used either X or Facebook?  What percentage have used neither?
			</p>
			<p>
				Let <m>X</m> be the set of all people who have used X, and <m>F</m> be the set of all people who have used Facebook.  Since we only know percentages, we let <m>n</m> represent the proportion of those surveyed in each set, so <m>n(F)=0.70</m> and <m>n(X)=0.40</m>. It is tempting to simply add these two numbers together to compute the size of <m>F \cup X</m>, but this is <em>incorrect</em> because doing so would count those who use both services twice.  
				To correct for this, we can add <m>n(X)</m> to <m>n(F)</m> and then subtract those in the intersection that have been added twice. That is,
				<md>
					<mrow>n(F\cup X) =\amp n(F) + n(X) - n(F\cap X) </mrow>
					<mrow> =\amp 0.70+0.40-0.20 </mrow>
					<mrow> =\amp 0.90</mrow>
				</md>
				Thus, 90% of the people surveyed have used Facebook or X.
			</p>
			<p>
				To find how many people have <em>not</em> used either service, we want the cardinality of <m>(F \cup X)^C</m>. Since <m>U</m> is 100%, and <m>n(F\cup X) = 0.9</m>, it follows that <m>n((F\cup X)^C) = 1.0 - 0.9 = 0.1</m>, or 10% of those surveyed. These quantities are illustrated in the Venn diagram below.
			</p>
			<image xml:id="img-2venn-social-media" width="43%">
				<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are two overlapping circles inside. One is labeled F and contains the number 0.50. The other is labeled X and contains the number 0.20. The intersection of the two circles contains the number 0.20. The region outside both circles contains the number 0.10.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2/7,font=\figfont]
						\draw[semithick,fill=white] (0,0) rectangle (21,14);
						% Sets: Path is fill, draw is boundary only
						\path[fill=white] (7,7) circle (5);
						\path[fill=yellow!50] (14,7) circle (5);
						\draw[semithick] (7,7) circle (5);
						\draw[semithick] (14,7) circle (5);
						% Set names
						\node at (7,11) {$F$};
						\node at (14,11) {$X$};
						% Numbers in each region
						\node at (6,7) {0.50};       % Only in F
						\node at (10.5,7) {0.20};    % Intersection
						\node at (15,7) {0.20};      % Only in X
						\node at (19,1.5) {0.10};   % Complement of union
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
			</image>
		</example>
		<fact xml:id="fact-sets-inclusion-exclusion-rule">
			<title>Inclusion-Exclusion Rule</title>
			<idx>inclusion-exclusion rule</idx>
			<idx><h>sets</h><h>inclusion-exclusion rule</h></idx>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two finite sets. Then
					<md>
						n(A\cup B) = n(A) + n(B) - n(A\cap B).
					</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</fact>
		<p>
			(This is also called the <term>addition law</term><idx><h>addition law</h><see>inclusion-exclusion rule</see></idx>; its probability version is the <xref ref="fact-probability-rule-general-addition" text="custom">general addition rule</xref> in <xref ref="sec-probability-basics"/>. It is not the same as the <xref ref="principle-counting-rule-addition-informal" text="custom">addition rule</xref> in <xref ref="sec-counting-addition-multiplication-rules"/>, which applies only when the sets do not overlap.)
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-mutually-exclusive">
	  	<idx>mutually exclusive</idx>
	  	<idx><h>sets</h><h>mutually exclusive</h></idx>
	  	<statement>
	  		<p>
					Two sets, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, are <term>mutually exclusive</term> if their intersection is empty.  That is, <m>A\cap B = \emptyset</m>, which means that <m>n(A\cap B) = 0</m>. In this case, the <xref text="title" ref="fact-sets-inclusion-exclusion-rule"/> simplifies to <m>n(A\cup B) = n(A) + n(B)</m>.
				</p>
	  	</statement>
	  </definition>
		<p>
			A two-set Venn diagram can be solved for any of the four regions found in such a diagram, as illustrated by the next example.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					At a movie theater, 200 people bought popcorn and 150 bought soda. If 90 people bought both popcorn and soda, how many people bought popcorn but no soda?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>P</m> represent the set of people who bought popcorn, and <m>S</m> represent the set of people who bought soda. Then we are told that <m>n(P)=200</m>, <m>n(S)=150</m>, and <m>n(P\cap S) = 90</m>. 
					The number of people who bought popcorn but no soda is the cardinality of <m>P\cap S^C</m>. This is the part of the Venn diagram inside of <m>P</m> but outside of <m>S</m>. Note that
					<md>
						n\left(P\cap S^C\right) = n(P)-n(P\cap S) = 200-90 = 110.
					</md>
					That is, 110 people bought popcorn but not soda.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-2venn-popcorn" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are two overlapping circles inside. One is labeled P and contains the number 110. The other is labeled S and contains the number 60. The intersection of the two circles contains the number 90.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2/7,font=\figfont]
							\draw[semithick,fill=white] (0,0) rectangle (21,14);
							% Sets: Path is fill, draw is boundary only
							\path[fill=white] (7,7) circle (5);
							\path[fill=yellow!50] (14,7) circle (5);
							\draw[semithick] (7,7) circle (5);
							\draw[semithick] (14,7) circle (5);
							% Set names
							\node at (7,11) {$P$};
							\node at (14,11) {$S$};
							% Numbers in each region
							\node at (6,7) {110};      % Only in P
							\node at (10.5,7) {90};   % Intersection
							\node at (15,7) {60};     % Only in S
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection>
		<title>Counting Problems with Three Sets</title>
		<p>
			Counting problems involving three sets are solved similarly to those involving two sets. The primary difference is that while two sets divide the universal set into four potential regions, three sets divide the universal set into eight total regions as shown in <xref ref="fig-3-set-venn-diagram"/>. 
			Computing the cardinality of these regions is thus more complex than for two-set problems. As we shall see in the following examples, it requires care to tease out correct values without double (or triple!) counting elements.
			Note that the <xref text="title" ref="fact-sets-inclusion-exclusion-rule"/> can be updated for three-set problems, but we do not include the formula here.
		</p>
		<p>
			To compute cardinality in three-set counting problems, begin by finding the central intersection where all three sets overlap, if possible. Then, calculate the regions where exactly two sets overlap. 
			Using these values, determine how many elements belong uniquely to each individual set. Finally, add all these regions together and verify that they match the given total, or use the total to compute any missing values. The following examples demonstrate this approach.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Suppose that fifty students are surveyed and asked if they would be taking a science <m>(S)</m>, a humanities <m>(H)</m>, or a religion <m>(R)</m> course next quarter. The results are:
					<ul>
						<li>
							<p> 21 were taking a science course,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p> 26 were taking a humanities course,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p> 19 were taking a religion course,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	9 were taking science and humanities,	</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	7 were taking science and religion,	</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	10 were taking humanities and religion,	</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p> 3 were taking all three,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	7 were taking none of these classes. </p>
						</li>
					</ul>
					How many students are only taking a science course? 
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="0% 0%" valigns="top top">
					<p>
						To answer this question, it helps to create a Venn diagram that shows all the ways the three sets could overlap. We then work through the given survey results and use them to find the cardinality of each region of the diagram, starting at the center and working outward. When working by hand, we simply fill in the regions with the computed cardinality. However, to help understand the process, in this example we will refer to regions by the letters shown in the provided image. 
					</p>
					<figure xml:id="fig-3-set-venn-diagram">
						<caption>A three-set Venn diagram divides the plane into 8 distinct regions, here marked with <m>a</m> through <m>h</m>.</caption>
						<image xml:id="img-3venn-courses" width="94%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are three overlapping circles inside. One is labeled S, one H, and one R. The distinct regions are labeled with lower case letters a through h.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[
										tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
										labelA={},
										labelB={},
										labelC={},
										labelOnlyA={$a$},
										labelOnlyB={$c$},
										labelOnlyC={$g$},
										labelOnlyAB={$b$},
										labelOnlyAC={$d$},
										labelOnlyBC={$f$},
										labelABC={$e$},
										labelNotABC={$h$},
								]

								% Add shading for subregions
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=yellow!25}
								\fillOnlyA
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=cyan!25}
								\fillOnlyB
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=magenta!25}
								\fillOnlyC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=green!15}
								\fillACapBNotC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=orange!15}
								\fillACapCNotB
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=violet!25}
								\fillBCapCNotA
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=white!25}
								\fillACapBCapC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=white}
								\fillNotABC

								% Add labels outside the circles
								\node at (.5, 4.25) {$S$}; % S above and left of set A
								\node at (4.5, 4.25) {$H$};  % H above and right of set B
								\node at (1.75, 0.33) {$R$}; % R below and left of set C

								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</figure>
				</sidebyside>
				<p>
					From the given data, we know that there are 3 students in region <m>e</m> and 7 students in region <m>h</m>. Since 7 students were taking a science and a religion course, we know that <m>n(d) + n(e) = 7</m>.  Since we know there are 3 students in region <m>e</m>, there must be <m>7 - 3 = 4</m> students in region <m>d</m>. 
				</p>
				<p>
					Similarly, because there are 10 students taking both humanities and religion, which includes regions <m>e</m> and <m>f</m>, there must be <m>10 - 3 = 7</m> students in region <m>f</m>.  With 9 students taking science and humanities courses, there must be <m>9 - 3 = 6</m> students in region <m>b</m>. 
				</p>
				<p>
					Finally, we know that 21 students were taking a science course.  This includes students from regions <m>a,\ b,\ d</m>, and <m>e</m>.  Since we know the number of students in all but region <m>a</m>, we can determine that <m>n(a) = 21 -6 -4 -3 = 8</m>. That is, 8 students are only taking a science course.
				</p>
				<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="0% 0%" valigns="top top">
					<p>
						<alert>Note:</alert> The above work was directed at finding the number of students who were only taking a science class. However, it is a good idea to <q>solve</q> the three-set diagram completely if possible. 
						Once this is done, any question about cardinality can be solved quickly. In this case, we can find that <m>n(c)=26-6-3-7=10</m>, and <m>n(g)=19-4-3-7=5</m>. The solved diagram is shown here.
					</p>
					<figure xml:id="fig-3-set-venn-diagram-solved">
						<caption>The <q>solved</q> three-set Venn diagram showing the cardinality of each region.</caption>
						<image xml:id="img-3venn-courses-solved" width="100%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are three overlapping circles inside. One is labeled S, one H, and one R. The distinct regions are labeled with numbers representing their cardinality.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[
										tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
										labelA={},
										labelB={},
										labelC={},
										labelOnlyA={8},
										labelOnlyB={10},
										labelOnlyC={5},
										labelOnlyAB={6},
										labelOnlyAC={4},
										labelOnlyBC={7},
										labelABC={3},
										labelNotABC={7},
								]

								% Add shading for subregions
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=yellow!25}
								\fillOnlyA
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=cyan!25}
								\fillOnlyB
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=magenta!25}
								\fillOnlyC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=green!15}
								\fillACapBNotC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=orange!15}
								\fillACapCNotB
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=violet!25}
								\fillBCapCNotA
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=white!25}
								\fillACapBCapC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=white}
								\fillNotABC

								% Add labels outside the circles
								\node at (.5, 4.25) {$S$}; % S above and left of set A
								\node at (4.5, 4.25) {$H$};  % H above and right of set B
								\node at (1.75, 0.33) {$R$}; % R below and left of set C

								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</figure>
				</sidebyside>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					In a survey of 75 consumers, 12 indicated they were going to buy a new couch, 18 said they were going to buy a new rug, and 24 said they were going to buy a new lamp. Of these, 6 were going to buy both a couch and a rug, 4 were going to buy a couch and a lamp, and 10 were going to buy a lamp and a rug. One person indicated they were going to buy all three.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p> How many were going to buy none of these items?</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	How many were going to buy only a couch? </p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	How many were going to buy only a lamp?	</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	How many were going to buy only a rug? </p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	How many were going to buy a lamp and a rug but not a couch? </p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
				 Label the sets of people buying couches, rugs, and lamps by <m>C</m>, <m>R</m>, and <m>L</m>. Let <m>U</m> denote the universal set. Then we know from the given problem statement that 
				 <md>
					<mrow> n(C) =\amp 12, </mrow>
					<mrow> n(R) =\amp 18, </mrow>
					<mrow> n(L) =\amp 24, </mrow>
					<mrow> n(C\cap R) =\amp 6, </mrow>
					<mrow> n(C\cap L) =\amp 4, </mrow>
					<mrow> n(L\cap R) =\amp 10, </mrow>
					<mrow> n(C\cap R\cap L) =\amp 1, </mrow>
					<mrow> n(U) =\amp 75. </mrow>
				 </md>
				 Use this information to create a Venn diagram with three interlocking circles labeled <m>C</m>, <m>R</m>, and <m>L</m>. To begin, use the fact that <m>n(C\cap R\cap L) = 1</m> to fill in the center of the diagram.
				</p>
				<p>
					Since <m>n(C\cap R) = 6</m>, <m>n(C\cap L) = 4</m>, and <m>n(L\cap R) = 10</m>, we compute <m>6-1=5</m> and place it in the proper region (giving a total of 6 in the set <m>C\cap R</m>). Similarly, we place 3 and 9 in the proper regions for the sets <m>C\cap L</m> and <m>L\cap R</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Next, <m>n(C)=12</m>, and 9 of these 12 are accounted for, so 3 remain. In the same way, <m>n(R)=18</m> with 15 accounted for, so 3 remain. For <m>n(L)=24</m>, 13 are accounted for and 11 remain.
				</p>
				<p>
					Finally, the number in <m>(C\cup R\cup L)^C</m> is the total of 75 less those accounted for in <m>C\cup R\cup L</m>. That is,
					<md>
						<mrow> n((C\cup R\cup L)^C) =\amp 75-(3+5+1+3+3+9+11) </mrow>
						<mrow> =\amp 75-35 =40. </mrow>
					</md>
					These values are shown in the figure below.
				</p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-3-set-venn-diagram-consumer">
					<caption>The <q>solved</q> three-set Venn diagram showing the cardinality of each region.</caption>
					<image xml:id="img-3venn-consumers-solved" width="45%">
						<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are three overlapping circles inside. One is labeled C, one R, and one L. The distinct regions are labeled with numbers representing their cardinality.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{venndiagram3sets}[
								tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$C$},
							labelB={$R$},
							labelC={$L$},
							labelOnlyA={3},
							labelOnlyB={3},
							labelOnlyC={11},
							labelOnlyAB={5},
							labelOnlyAC={3},
							labelOnlyBC={9},
							labelABC={1},
							labelNotABC={40},
							]
					
							% Add shading for subregions
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=yellow!25}
							\fillOnlyA
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=cyan!25}
							\fillOnlyB
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=magenta!25}
							\fillOnlyC
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=green!15}
							\fillACapBNotC
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=orange!15}
							\fillACapCNotB
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=violet!25}
							\fillBCapCNotA
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=white!25}
							\fillACapBCapC
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=white}
							\fillNotABC
					
							% Add label for U
							\node at (4.75, 4.75) {$U$};
					
							\end{venndiagram3sets}
						</latex-image>
					</image>
				</figure>
				<p>
					From this figure, we can conclude that
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p> 40 were going to buy none of these items. </p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	3 were going to buy only a couch. </p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	11 were going to buy only a lamp.	</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	3 were going to buy only a rug. </p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>	9 were going to buy a lamp and a rug but not a couch. </p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			Sometimes the given information does not allow us to fill in every region by working outward from the center. This happens when one of the two-set overlaps is not given, because then neither of the two single-set regions next to it can be computed either.
			In this case, we represent the unknown region by a variable such as <m>x</m>, write the neighboring regions in terms of <m>x</m>, and use the total number of elements to set up an equation and solve for <m>x</m>.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A farmers market surveyed 75 shoppers about what they bought that morning. The results are:
					<ul>
						<li>
							<p>46 bought vegetables,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>34 bought baked goods,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>22 bought flowers,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>12 bought vegetables and flowers,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>9 bought baked goods and flowers,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>5 bought all three,</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>8 bought none of these items.</p>
						</li>
					</ul>
					How many shoppers bought vegetables and baked goods?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="0% 0%" valigns="top top">
					<p>
						Label the sets of shoppers who bought vegetables, baked goods, and flowers by <m>V</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>F</m>. As before, we start at the center: 5 shoppers bought all three items. Since 12 bought vegetables and flowers, <m>12 - 5 = 7</m> bought only those two, and since 9 bought baked goods and flowers, <m>9 - 5 = 4</m> bought only those two. That leaves <m>22 - 5 - 7 - 4 = 6</m> who bought only flowers, and 8 shoppers are outside all three circles.
					</p>
					<figure xml:id="fig-3-set-venn-diagram-market">
						<caption>The three-set Venn diagram with the unknown region labeled <m>x</m>.</caption>
						<image xml:id="img-3venn-market-unknown" width="94%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are three overlapping circles inside, labeled V, B, and F. The regions are labeled with numbers or with expressions involving x.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[
								tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
								labelA={$V$},
								labelB={$B$},
								labelC={$F$},
								labelOnlyA={$34-x$},
								labelOnlyB={$25-x$},
								labelOnlyC={6},
								labelOnlyAB={$x$},
								labelOnlyAC={7},
								labelOnlyBC={4},
								labelABC={5},
								labelNotABC={8},
								]

								% Add shading for subregions
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=yellow!25}
								\fillOnlyA
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=cyan!25}
								\fillOnlyB
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=magenta!25}
								\fillOnlyC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=green!15}
								\fillACapBNotC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=orange!15}
								\fillACapCNotB
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=violet!25}
								\fillBCapCNotA
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=white!25}
								\fillACapBCapC
								\setkeys{venn}{shade=white}
								\fillNotABC

								% Add label for U
								\node at (4.75, 4.75) {$U$};

								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</figure>
				</sidebyside>
				<p>
					We are not told how many shoppers bought vegetables and baked goods, so we cannot find the region for <m>V</m> and <m>B</m> only. Without it, we also cannot find the regions for <m>V</m> only or <m>B</m> only. Instead, let <m>x</m> be the number of shoppers who bought vegetables and baked goods but not flowers.
					Since 46 shoppers bought vegetables, the number who bought only vegetables is <m>46 - x - 5 - 7 = 34 - x</m>. Similarly, the number who bought only baked goods is <m>34 - x - 5 - 4 = 25 - x</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					All 75 shoppers belong to exactly one of the eight regions, so the regions must add up to 75:
					<md>
						<mrow> (34-x) + (25-x) + 6 + x + 7 + 4 + 5 + 8 =\amp 75 </mrow>
						<mrow> 89 - x =\amp 75 </mrow>
						<mrow> x =\amp 14. </mrow>
					</md>
					So 14 shoppers bought vegetables and baked goods but not flowers. The question asks for everyone who bought vegetables and baked goods, which also includes the 5 who bought all three. Therefore <m>14 + 5 = 19</m> shoppers bought vegetables and baked goods.
				</p>
				<p>
					With <m>x = 14</m>, we can also find that 20 shoppers bought only vegetables and 11 bought only baked goods. The solved diagram is shown below.
				</p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-3-set-venn-diagram-market-solved">
					<caption>The <q>solved</q> three-set Venn diagram showing the cardinality of each region.</caption>
					<image xml:id="img-3venn-market-solved" width="45%">
						<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U. There are three overlapping circles inside, labeled V, B, and F. The distinct regions are labeled with numbers representing their cardinality.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{venndiagram3sets}[
							tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$V$},
							labelB={$B$},
							labelC={$F$},
							labelOnlyA={20},
							labelOnlyB={11},
							labelOnlyC={6},
							labelOnlyAB={14},
							labelOnlyAC={7},
							labelOnlyBC={4},
							labelABC={5},
							labelNotABC={8},
							]

							% Add shading for subregions
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=yellow!25}
							\fillOnlyA
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=cyan!25}
							\fillOnlyB
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=magenta!25}
							\fillOnlyC
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=green!15}
							\fillACapBNotC
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=orange!15}
							\fillACapCNotB
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=violet!25}
							\fillBCapCNotA
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=white!25}
							\fillACapBCapC
							\setkeys{venn}{shade=white}
							\fillNotABC

							% Add label for U
							\node at (4.75, 4.75) {$U$};

							\end{venndiagram3sets}
						</latex-image>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<exercises>
		<!-- Sources: Exercises 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 12, 13, and 14 are adapted
		from Benjamin Jackson's MATH 105 warm-up, lecture slides, clicker
		questions, and practice sheet (Objectives 10 and 11), Walla Walla
		University, Fall 2026. Exercises 4, 9, 10, 15, and 16 are based on
		his MATH 105 written-homework question library (Fall 2026).
		Exercises 7, 11, and 17 are new.
		-->
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-pizza-tacos"><!--  #1 (Obj 10 warm-up: That Doesn't Add Up) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Noah surveys his class of 30 students and finds that 19 like pizza and 17 like tacos. He notices that <m>19 + 17 = 36</m>, but there are only 30 students in the class.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How can both of Noah's counts be correct?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If every student likes at least one of the two foods, how many like both?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								If 3 students like neither food, how many like both?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>P</m> be the students who like pizza and <m>T</m> the students who like tacos.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who like both foods are counted once among the 19 and again among the 17, so the sum 36 counts them twice. Nothing is wrong with either count.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Here <m>n(P \cup T) = 30</m>, so by the inclusion-exclusion rule <m>30 = 19 + 17 - n(P \cap T)</m>, and <m>n(P \cap T) = 36 - 30 = 6</m> students like both.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Now <m>n(P \cup T) = 30 - 3 = 27</m>, so <m>27 = 19 + 17 - n(P \cap T)</m>, and <m>n(P \cap T) = 36 - 27 = 9</m> students like both.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-hockey-helmets"><!--  #2 (Obj 10 slides: hockey injury data; new questions) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					The table below shows injuries to hockey players who wore half-shield or full-shield helmets. Let <m>H</m> and <m>F</m> be the sets of injuries to players wearing half shields and full shields, and let <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>D</m> be the sets of head and face injuries, concussions, neck injuries, and other injuries.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How many injuries were head or face injuries or happened to players wearing half shields?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Find <m>n(C \cup F)</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Write <q>neck injuries to players who were not wearing half shields</q> in set notation, and find the number of such injuries.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Explain why <m>n(A \cap B) = 0</m> for this table, and find <m>n(A \cup B)</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell></cell>
						<cell>Half Shield (<m>H</m>)</cell>
						<cell>Full Shield (<m>F</m>)</cell>
						<cell>Totals</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell halign="left">Head and Face (<m>A</m>)</cell>
						<cell>95</cell>
						<cell>34</cell>
						<cell>129</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell halign="left">Concussions (<m>B</m>)</cell>
						<cell>41</cell>
						<cell>38</cell>
						<cell>79</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell halign="left">Neck Injuries (<m>C</m>)</cell>
						<cell>9</cell>
						<cell>7</cell>
						<cell>16</cell>
					</row>
					<row bottom="minor">
						<cell halign="left">Other Injuries (<m>D</m>)</cell>
						<cell>202</cell>
						<cell>150</cell>
						<cell>352</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell halign="left">Totals</cell>
						<cell>347</cell>
						<cell>229</cell>
						<cell>576</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(A \cup H) = n(A) + n(H) - n(A \cap H) = 129 + 347 - 95 = 381</m> injuries. The 95 head and face injuries with half shields are subtracted because they were counted in both 129 and 347.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(C \cup F) = 16 + 229 - 7 = 238</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>C \cap H^C</m>. Every player wore one of the two helmets, so <m>H^C = F</m>, and <m>n(C \cap H^C) = n(C \cap F) = 7</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Each injury is recorded in exactly one row, so no injury is both a head or face injury and a concussion: <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are mutually exclusive. Then <m>n(A \cup B) = n(A) + n(B) = 129 + 79 = 208</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-reading-regions"><!--  #3 (Obj 10 slides: Practice with Regions; new numbers) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use the Venn diagram to find each cardinality.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-reading-regions" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains two overlapping circles labeled A and B. A only contains 9, the overlap contains 6, B only contains 14, and the region outside both circles contains 11.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram2sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$A$}, labelB={$B$},
							labelOnlyA={9}, labelAB={6}, labelOnlyB={14}, labelNotAB={11}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram2sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)" cols="2">
						<li><p><m>n(A)</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(B)</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A \cup B)</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A^C \cap B)</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A \cap B^C)</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(U)</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>n((A \cup B)^C)</m></p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A^C)</m></p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>n(A) = 9 + 6 = 15</m>. The set <m>A</m> includes the overlap, not just the 9.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(B) = 6 + 14 = 20</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A \cup B) = 9 + 6 + 14 = 29</m>. (Check: <m>15 + 20 - 6 = 29</m>.)</p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A^C \cap B) = 14</m>, the part of <m>B</m> outside <m>A</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A \cap B^C) = 9</m>, the part of <m>A</m> outside <m>B</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(U) = 9 + 6 + 14 + 11 = 40</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>n((A \cup B)^C) = 11</m>, or <m>40 - 29</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>n(A^C) = 14 + 11 = 25</m>, or <m>40 - 15</m>.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-inclusion-exclusion-drill"><!--  #4 (based on written 10a) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use the inclusion-exclusion rule to answer each question.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Find <m>n(A \cup B)</m> if <m>n(A) = 14</m>, <m>n(B) = 11</m>, and <m>n(A \cap B) = 5</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Find <m>n(A \cap B)</m> if <m>n(A \cup B) = 30</m>, <m>n(A) = 18</m>, and <m>n(B) = 19</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Find <m>n(B)</m> if <m>n(A \cup B) = 25</m>, <m>n(A) = 12</m>, and <m>n(A \cap B) = 4</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Is it possible to have <m>n(A) = 10</m>, <m>n(B) = 8</m>, and <m>n(A \cup B) = 20</m>? Explain.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(A \cup B) = 14 + 11 - 5 = 20</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>30 = 18 + 19 - n(A \cap B)</m>, so <m>n(A \cap B) = 37 - 30 = 7</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>25 = 12 + n(B) - 4</m>, so <m>25 = 8 + n(B)</m> and <m>n(B) = 17</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								No. Solving <m>20 = 10 + 8 - n(A \cap B)</m> gives <m>n(A \cap B) = -2</m>, which is impossible. The union can have at most <m>10 + 8 = 18</m> elements, which happens when the sets are mutually exclusive.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-cardio-machines"><!--  #5 (Obj 10 clicker: gym usage; new machines and numbers) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A fitness club surveyed 300 of its members about the cardio machines they use regularly. They found that 185 use a treadmill, 140 use a rowing machine, and 72 use both.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How many use a treadmill or a rowing machine?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many use exactly one of the two machines?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many use neither machine?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>T</m> be the members who use a treadmill and <m>R</m> the members who use a rowing machine.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(T \cup R) = 185 + 140 - 72 = 253</m> members. (Adding <m>185 + 140 = 325</m> counts the 72 who use both twice.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>185 - 72 = 113</m> use only a treadmill and <m>140 - 72 = 68</m> use only a rowing machine, so <m>113 + 68 = 181</m> use exactly one.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>300 - 253 = 47</m> members use neither.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-two-sales"><!--  #6 (Obj 10 slides: Shopping; new numbers) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					An online store surveyed 400 customers. Of these, 240 made a purchase during its spring sale, 210 made a purchase during its fall sale, and 360 made a purchase during at least one of the two sales.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How many customers made a purchase during both sales?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Draw a Venn diagram and write the number of customers in each of its four regions.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Jordan answers (a) with <m>240 + 210 - 400 = 50</m>. Explain the mistake.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>S</m> and <m>F</m> be the customers who bought during the spring and fall sales.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(S \cup F) = 360</m>, so <m>360 = 240 + 210 - n(S \cap F)</m>, and <m>n(S \cap F) = 450 - 360 = 90</m> customers.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Spring only: <m>240 - 90 = 150</m>. Fall only: <m>210 - 90 = 120</m>. Neither: <m>400 - 360 = 40</m>. See the diagram below.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The inclusion-exclusion rule needs <m>n(S \cup F)</m>, which is 360, not 400. The 40 customers who bought at neither sale are in the universal set but not in <m>S \cup F</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-two-sales" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains two overlapping circles labeled S and F. S only contains 150, the overlap contains 90, F only contains 120, and the region outside both circles contains 40.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram2sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$S$}, labelB={$F$},
							labelOnlyA={150}, labelAB={90}, labelOnlyB={120}, labelNotAB={40}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram2sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-commuters"><!--  #7 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A city surveyed 150 commuters. Of these, 45 ride only the bus, 30 ride only light rail, and 25 ride both the bus and light rail.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How many commuters ride the bus?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many ride light rail?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many ride neither?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The numbers given are regions of the Venn diagram, so nothing needs to be subtracted.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>45 + 25 = 70</m> ride the bus. (Not 45: those are the commuters who ride <em>only</em> the bus.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>30 + 25 = 55</m> ride light rail.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>150 - (45 + 25 + 30) = 50</m> ride neither.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-concert-merch"><!--  #8 (Obj 10 slides: Movie Snacks; new context and numbers) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					At a concert, 320 people bought a T-shirt and 140 bought a poster. Of these, 60 bought both.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How many people bought a T-shirt but no poster?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many bought a poster but no T-shirt?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Can you find how many people at the concert bought neither? Explain.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>T</m> be the people who bought a T-shirt and <m>P</m> the people who bought a poster.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(T \cap P^C) = 320 - 60 = 260</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(P \cap T^C) = 140 - 60 = 80</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								No. We know <m>n(T \cup P) = 320 + 140 - 60 = 400</m>, but finding the number who bought neither requires the total attendance, <m>n(U)</m>, which is not given.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-national-parks"><!--  #9 (based on written 10b) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A survey of 120 people at a trailhead found that 64 have visited Yellowstone National Park, 38 have visited Glacier National Park, and 15 have visited both.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Draw a Venn diagram of the survey results, writing the number of people in each of its four regions.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many have visited Yellowstone but not Glacier?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many have visited exactly one of the two parks?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many have visited neither park?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>Y</m> and <m>G</m> be the people who have visited Yellowstone and Glacier.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Start with the overlap, 15. Then <m>64 - 15 = 49</m> are in <m>Y</m> only and <m>38 - 15 = 23</m> are in <m>G</m> only. The rest, <m>120 - (49 + 15 + 23) = 33</m>, are outside both circles. See the diagram below.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(Y \cap G^C) = 49</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>49 + 23 = 72</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(Y \cup G) = 64 + 38 - 15 = 87</m>, so <m>120 - 87 = 33</m> have visited neither.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-national-parks" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains two overlapping circles labeled Y and G. Y only contains 49, the overlap contains 15, G only contains 23, and the region outside both circles contains 33.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram2sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$Y$}, labelB={$G$},
							labelOnlyA={49}, labelAB={15}, labelOnlyB={23}, labelNotAB={33}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram2sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-jobs-intramurals"><!--  #10 (based on written 10c) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A survey of the 45 students on one floor of a residence hall found that 28 have a part-time job, 21 play an intramural sport, and 5 do neither.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								How many students have a job or play an intramural sport?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use the inclusion-exclusion rule to find how many students have a job and play an intramural sport.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								How many play an intramural sport but do not have a job?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>J</m> be the students with a job and <m>S</m> the students who play an intramural sport, so <m>n(U) = 45</m>.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Everyone except the 5 who do neither: <m>n(J \cup S) = 45 - 5 = 40</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>40 = 28 + 21 - n(J \cap S)</m>, so <m>n(J \cap S) = 49 - 40 = 9</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(S \cap J^C) = 21 - 9 = 12</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-jobs-intramurals" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains two overlapping circles labeled J and S. J only contains 19, the overlap contains 9, S only contains 12, and the region outside both circles contains 5.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram2sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$J$}, labelB={$S$},
							labelOnlyA={19}, labelAB={9}, labelOnlyB={12}, labelNotAB={5}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram2sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-music-streaming"><!--  #11 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					In a survey of college students, 55% use Spotify, 35% use Apple Music, and 15% use both.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								What percentage use at least one of the two services?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								What percentage use neither?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								What percentage use exactly one of the two services?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>S</m> and <m>A</m> be the students who use Spotify and Apple Music, and let <m>n</m> represent the proportion of students in each set.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>n(S \cup A) = 0.55 + 0.35 - 0.15 = 0.75</m>, or 75%.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>1 - 0.75 = 0.25</m>, or 25%.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Spotify only is <m>0.55 - 0.15 = 0.40</m> and Apple Music only is <m>0.35 - 0.15 = 0.20</m>, so 60% use exactly one.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-naming-regions"><!--  #12 (Obj 11 clickers: regions of S, H, R; more parts) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Fifty students are asked whether they are taking a science <m>(S)</m>, humanities <m>(H)</m>, or religion <m>(R)</m> course next quarter. The diagram labels the eight regions <m>a</m> through <m>h</m>. List the region(s) that represent each set.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-naming-regions" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains three overlapping circles labeled S (upper left), H (upper right), and R (bottom). S only is region a, S and H only is b, H only is c, S and R only is d, all three is e, H and R only is f, R only is g, and the region outside all three circles is h.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram3sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$S$}, labelB={$H$}, labelC={$R$},
							labelOnlyA={$a$}, labelOnlyAB={$b$}, labelOnlyB={$c$},
							labelOnlyAC={$d$}, labelABC={$e$}, labelOnlyBC={$f$},
							labelOnlyC={$g$}, labelNotABC={$h$}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram3sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>S \cap H</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Students taking a science or a humanities course</p></li>
						<li><p><m>S \cap H \cap R^C</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Students taking only a humanities course</p></li>
						<li><p><m>R \cap S^C</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Students taking exactly two of the three kinds of courses</p></li>
						<li><p><m>(S \cup H \cup R)^C</m></p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>b</m> and <m>e</m>. The center, <m>e</m>, is in <m>S</m> and <m>H</m> (and also <m>R</m>).</p></li>
						<li><p><m>a, b, c, d, e, f</m>. <q>Or</q> means the union <m>S \cup H</m>, everything in either circle.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>b</m> only.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>c</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>f</m> and <m>g</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>b, d, f</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>h</m>.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-deans-list"><!--  #13 (Obj 11 slides and practice sheet: Small Colleges; new numbers) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					At a small college:
					<ul>
						<li><p>28 female seniors were on the dean's list,</p></li>
						<li><p>54 women were on the dean's list who were not seniors,</p></li>
						<li><p>40 male seniors were on the dean's list,</p></li>
						<li><p>75 female seniors were not on the dean's list,</p></li>
						<li><p>82 male seniors were not on the dean's list,</p></li>
						<li><p>240 women were not seniors and were not on the dean's list,</p></li>
						<li><p>71 men were on the dean's list who were not seniors,</p></li>
						<li><p>210 men were not seniors and were not on the dean's list.</p></li>
					</ul>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>How many students were seniors?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many were women?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many were on the dean's list?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many seniors were on the dean's list?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many women were on the dean's list?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many were men?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many students attended the college?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Let <m>S</m> be the seniors, <m>W</m> the women, and <m>D</m> the students on the dean's list; men are <m>W^C</m>. Each fact describes exactly one of the eight regions, so nothing needs to be subtracted. For example, <q>54 women were on the dean's list who were not seniors</q> is the region <m>W \cap D \cap S^C</m>.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-deans-list" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains three overlapping circles labeled S, W, and D. S only contains 82, W only 240, D only 71; S and W only 75, S and D only 40, W and D only 54; all three 28; outside all three circles 210.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram3sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$S$}, labelB={$W$}, labelC={$D$},
							labelOnlyA={82}, labelOnlyAB={75}, labelOnlyB={240},
							labelOnlyAC={40}, labelABC={28}, labelOnlyBC={54},
							labelOnlyC={71}, labelNotABC={210}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram3sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>28 + 40 + 75 + 82 = 225</m> seniors.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>28 + 54 + 75 + 240 = 397</m> women.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>28 + 54 + 40 + 71 = 193</m> students on the dean's list.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>28 + 40 = 68</m> seniors on the dean's list.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>28 + 54 = 82</m> women on the dean's list.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>40 + 82 + 71 + 210 = 403</m> men.</p></li>
						<li><p>All eight regions: <m>397 + 403 = 800</m> students.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-news-sources"><!--  #14 (Obj 11 slides and practice sheet: Magazines; new sets and numbers) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A survey of 90 college students asked whether they get their news from a news app <m>(N)</m>, from podcasts <m>(P)</m>, or from TV news <m>(T)</m>. The results are:
					<ul>
						<li><p>30 use a news app,</p></li>
						<li><p>24 listen to podcasts,</p></li>
						<li><p>40 do not watch TV news,</p></li>
						<li><p>14 use a news app but do not listen to podcasts,</p></li>
						<li><p>13 listen to podcasts and watch TV news,</p></li>
						<li><p>15 use a news app and watch TV news,</p></li>
						<li><p>6 use all three.</p></li>
					</ul>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>How many students use none of the three?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many watch only TV news?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many use a news app and listen to podcasts but do not watch TV news?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many use exactly two of the three?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Fill in the Venn diagram from the center out.
					<ul>
						<li><p>All three: 6.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>P</m> and <m>T</m> only: <m>13 - 6 = 7</m>. <m>N</m> and <m>T</m> only: <m>15 - 6 = 9</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>N</m> only: the 14 in <m>N</m> but not <m>P</m> include the 9 in <m>N</m> and <m>T</m> only, so <m>14 - 9 = 5</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>N</m> and <m>P</m> only: <m>30 - 5 - 9 - 6 = 10</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>P</m> only: <m>24 - 10 - 6 - 7 = 1</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>T</m> only: <m>90 - 40 = 50</m> watch TV news, so <m>50 - 9 - 6 - 7 = 28</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>None: the 40 who do not watch TV news are <m>5 + 10 + 1 = 16</m> plus those who use none, so <m>40 - 16 = 24</m>.</p></li>
					</ul>
					Check: <m>5 + 10 + 1 + 9 + 6 + 7 + 28 + 24 = 90</m>.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-news-sources" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains three overlapping circles labeled N, P, and T. N only contains 5, P only 1, T only 28; N and P only 10, N and T only 9, P and T only 7; all three 6; outside all three circles 24.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram3sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$N$}, labelB={$P$}, labelC={$T$},
							labelOnlyA={5}, labelOnlyAB={10}, labelOnlyB={1},
							labelOnlyAC={9}, labelABC={6}, labelOnlyBC={7},
							labelOnlyC={28}, labelNotABC={24}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram3sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>24 students.</p></li>
						<li><p>28 students. (Not 50: that is everyone who watches TV news.)</p></li>
						<li><p>10 students. (Not <m>10 + 6 = 16</m>: the 6 in the center also watch TV news.)</p></li>
						<li><p><m>10 + 9 + 7 = 26</m> students.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-book-club"><!--  #15 (based on written 11b) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A library asked the members of its book club which genres they read: mystery <m>(M)</m>, fantasy <m>(F)</m>, or biography <m>(B)</m>. The results are:
					<ul>
						<li><p>25 read mystery,</p></li>
						<li><p>12 read fantasy,</p></li>
						<li><p>26 read biography,</p></li>
						<li><p>9 read mystery and fantasy,</p></li>
						<li><p>11 read mystery and biography,</p></li>
						<li><p>8 read fantasy and biography,</p></li>
						<li><p>5 read all three,</p></li>
						<li><p>4 read none of these genres.</p></li>
					</ul>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Complete a Venn diagram showing the number of members in each of the eight regions.</p></li>
						<li><p>How many members were surveyed?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many read exactly one of the three genres?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many read only fantasy?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many read at least two of the three genres?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								The center is 5. The two-genre-only regions are <m>9 - 5 = 4</m> (<m>M</m> and <m>F</m>), <m>11 - 5 = 6</m> (<m>M</m> and <m>B</m>), and <m>8 - 5 = 3</m> (<m>F</m> and <m>B</m>). Then mystery only is <m>25 - 4 - 6 - 5 = 10</m>, fantasy only is <m>12 - 4 - 3 - 5 = 0</m>, and biography only is <m>26 - 6 - 3 - 5 = 12</m>. Four members are outside all three circles. See the diagram below.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li><p><m>10 + 0 + 12 + 4 + 6 + 3 + 5 + 4 = 44</m> members.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>10 + 0 + 12 = 22</m> members.</p></li>
						<li><p>0 members. Every fantasy reader also reads at least one of the other genres.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>4 + 6 + 3 + 5 = 18</m> members.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-book-club" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains three overlapping circles labeled M, F, and B. M only contains 10, F only 0, B only 12; M and F only 4, M and B only 6, F and B only 3; all three 5; outside all three circles 4.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram3sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$M$}, labelB={$F$}, labelC={$B$},
							labelOnlyA={10}, labelOnlyAB={4}, labelOnlyB={0},
							labelOnlyAC={6}, labelABC={5}, labelOnlyBC={3},
							labelOnlyC={12}, labelNotABC={4}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram3sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-membership-tiers"><!--  #16 (based on written 11c) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					A recreation center offers three amenities: a pool <m>(P)</m>, group fitness classes <m>(C)</m>, and a weight room <m>(W)</m>. A membership with all three is called <em>Full Access</em>, a membership with classes and the weight room but not the pool is called <em>Fit</em>, and a membership with only the pool is called <em>Swim</em>. The center's records show:
					<ul>
						<li><p>28 memberships include the pool,</p></li>
						<li><p>13 include the pool but not classes,</p></li>
						<li><p>42 include classes,</p></li>
						<li><p>25 include classes and the weight room,</p></li>
						<li><p>50 include the weight room,</p></li>
						<li><p>15 include the pool and the weight room,</p></li>
						<li><p>10 include all three,</p></li>
						<li><p>6 include none of the three (social memberships).</p></li>
					</ul>
					How many memberships
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>are there in all?</p></li>
						<li><p>are Full Access?</p></li>
						<li><p>are Fit?</p></li>
						<li><p>are Swim?</p></li>
						<li><p>include exactly one of the three amenities?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Fill in the Venn diagram from the center out. The center is 10. Pool and weight room only: <m>15 - 10 = 5</m>. Classes and weight room only: <m>25 - 10 = 15</m>. The 13 with the pool but not classes are the pool-only region and the 5 with the pool and weight room only, so pool only is <m>13 - 5 = 8</m>. Then pool and classes only is <m>28 - 8 - 5 - 10 = 5</m>, classes only is <m>42 - 5 - 15 - 10 = 12</m>, and weight room only is <m>50 - 5 - 15 - 10 = 20</m>. Six are outside all three circles.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-membership-tiers" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains three overlapping circles labeled P, C, and W. P only contains 8, C only 12, W only 20; P and C only 5, P and W only 5, C and W only 15; all three 10; outside all three circles 6.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram3sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$P$}, labelB={$C$}, labelC={$W$},
							labelOnlyA={8}, labelOnlyAB={5}, labelOnlyB={12},
							labelOnlyAC={5}, labelABC={10}, labelOnlyBC={15},
							labelOnlyC={20}, labelNotABC={6}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram3sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>8 + 5 + 12 + 5 + 10 + 15 + 20 + 6 = 81</m> memberships.</p></li>
						<li><p>10 (the center).</p></li>
						<li><p>15 (classes and weight room only).</p></li>
						<li><p>8 (pool only).</p></li>
						<li><p><m>8 + 12 + 20 = 40</m>.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-outdoor-club"><!--  #17 (new; unknown region, like the farmers market example) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					An outdoor club with 80 members asked which activities members had done in the past year: trail running <m>(R)</m>, climbing <m>(C)</m>, or kayaking <m>(K)</m>. The results are:
					<ul>
						<li><p>38 went trail running,</p></li>
						<li><p>30 went climbing,</p></li>
						<li><p>33 went kayaking,</p></li>
						<li><p>14 went trail running and kayaking,</p></li>
						<li><p>11 went climbing and kayaking,</p></li>
						<li><p>4 did all three,</p></li>
						<li><p>9 did none of these activities.</p></li>
					</ul>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>How many members went trail running and climbing?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many went only climbing?</p></li>
						<li><p>How many did at least one of the three activities?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Start at the center, 4. Running and kayaking only: <m>14 - 4 = 10</m>. Climbing and kayaking only: <m>11 - 4 = 7</m>. Kayaking only: <m>33 - 4 - 10 - 7 = 12</m>. The number who went running and climbing is not given, so let <m>x</m> be the number who went running and climbing but not kayaking. Then running only is <m>38 - x - 4 - 10 = 24 - x</m> and climbing only is <m>30 - x - 4 - 7 = 19 - x</m>.
				</p>
				<p>
					All 80 members belong to exactly one of the eight regions:
					<md>
						<mrow> (24 - x) + (19 - x) + 12 + x + 10 + 7 + 4 + 9 =\amp 80 </mrow>
						<mrow> 85 - x =\amp 80 </mrow>
						<mrow> x =\amp 5. </mrow>
					</md>
					So running only is <m>24 - 5 = 19</m> and climbing only is <m>19 - 5 = 14</m>.
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-outdoor-club" width="45%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains three overlapping circles labeled R, C, and K. R only contains 19, C only 14, K only 12; R and C only 5, R and K only 10, C and K only 7; all three 4; outside all three circles 9.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{venndiagram3sets}[tikzoptions={font=\figfont},
							labelA={$R$}, labelB={$C$}, labelC={$K$},
							labelOnlyA={19}, labelOnlyAB={5}, labelOnlyB={14},
							labelOnlyAC={10}, labelABC={4}, labelOnlyBC={7},
							labelOnlyC={12}, labelNotABC={9}]
							\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
						\end{venndiagram3sets}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>x + 4 = 5 + 4 = 9</m> members. (The 5 is only those who did not also kayak.)</p></li>
						<li><p>14 members.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>80 - 9 = 71</m> members.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>